            <ArticleTitle>Two cases of pediatric essential thrombocythemia managed effectively with hydroxyurea.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>810-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s12185-012-1193-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Thrombocytosis is common in infancy and childhood. Essential thrombocythemia (ET), a myeloproliferative disorder, is a much less common cause of thrombocytosis in childhood. We report two cases of essential thrombocythemia in 5- and 10-year-old children, who presented with platelet counts of more than 1,000,000/mm(3). Treatment is not recommended for ET in an asymptomatic patient in the absence of bleeding or thrombosis and a platelet count &lt;1,500,000/mm(3). Our first case had platelets &gt;1,500,000/mm(3), and a second child was symptomatic with recurrent headache. Both responded well to therapy with hydroxyurea (dose 15-30 mg/kg/day) and tolerated it well.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
